      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Sodium cromoglicate (SCG), a chromone with anti-inflammatory, anti-itch and anti-allergic properties. We report a long-term study of a 4% aqueous solution of SCG in children with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis (AD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Children aged 1 to 12 years with AD were entered into a 12-week randomised clinical trial (RCT), followed by 12 months open treatment with known 4% SCG emulsion (Altocrom®). Primary endpoint was change in SCORAD score. Secondary endpoints included symptom severity, Quality of Life, concomitant treatment usage, global assessments."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "One hundred and seventy-seven subjects entered, 118 treated with 4% SCG emulsion and 59 with vehicle: 128 completed 12 months in open study. SCORAD score reduced during RCT by -15.3 (-33%) on 4% SCG emulsion and -18.0 (-39%) on vehicle: p\u2009=\u20090.2331. After 12 months reduction was 56%. No secondary endpoint showed differences between treatments during RCT. Thirty-two subjects reported treatment related events during RCT and open trial. Eleven (7%) reported application site discomfort. Most were reported as mild and most resolved without intervention and the study drug was stopped in one case only."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "SCG 4% cutaneous emulsion was well tolerated in children treated for 15 months."
        }
      ]},
